%[text] # Next Steps 
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_71425CA6] ## Advanced Control 
%[text] Beyond simple PID control, more advanced control techniques such as Model Predictive Control or Reinforcement Learning can also be used to operate robotic systems. These are largely beyond the scope of an introductory course like this, but some links for further exploration are given below to be explored at your own pace:
%[text] - [<u>**Model Predictive Control Examples**</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/mpc/examples.html)
%[text] - [<u>**Control of Quadrotor using Nonlinear MPC**</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/mpc/ug/control-of-quadrotor-using-nonlinear-model-predictive-control.html)
%[text] - [<u>**Reinforcement Learning Onramp**</u>](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/reinforcement-learning-onramp/reinforcementlearning)
%[text] - [<u>**Reinforcement Learning Examples**</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/reinforcement-learning/examples.html?category=index&exampleproduct=all) \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E212CEF3] ## Working with ROS & Gazebo
%[text] **What is ROS?**
%[text] The [<u>**Robot Operating System (ROS)**</u>](https://www.ros.org/) is a set of software libraries and tools that are commonly used for developing robotic applications. MATLAB has a [<u>**ROS Toolbox**</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/products/ros.html) that allows communication over both ROS1 and ROS2 networks. This is useful for integrating applications developed in MATLAB or Simulink with other simulators and hardware.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A connectivity diagram showing that ROS and ROS 2 connect to \n1. MATLAB & Simulink via the ROS Toolbox\n2. ROS Nodes in software\n3. Simulators and hardware](text:image:3c1b)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E1499645] - [**MATLAB and Simulink ROS Tutorials**](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/118630-matlab-and-simulink-ros-tutorials)
%[text] - [**Introduction to ROS**](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ros/gs/robot-operating-system-ros-basic-concepts_mw_0a63a5a4-2dcd-4e4e-a1ec-312c6ea06716.html)
%[text] - [**Introduction to ROS2**](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ros/gs/robot-operating-system-ros2-basic-concepts.html) \
%[text] The ROS Toolbox also allows co-simulation with the [<u>Gazebo Simulator</u>](https://gazebosim.org/), which is popularly used for robotics simulation: [<u>**Gazebo Co-Simulation Documentation**</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/robotics/gazebo-cosimulation.html)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E1499645] - [**Generate ROS Node for UAV Waypoint Follower**](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ros/ug/generate-a-standalone-ros-node-for-a-uav-waypoint-follower.html) \
%[text] We can also use the ROS Toolbox environment to create simulations of a UAV autopilot, such as PX4 hardware, using a virtual machine:
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E1499645] - [**Control a Simulated UAV using ROS 2**](https://www.mathworks.com/help/uav/ug/control-simulated-uav-using-ros2-px4-bridge.html) \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A connectivity diagram. The PX4 consists of PX4 Process and Client that communicate with uORB. The Virtual Machine consists of the PX4 and the Agent which communicate via UDP. And the Virtual Machine communicates with the ROS 2 Nodes in MATLAB via UPD.](text:image:8104)
%[text] Additionally, it is also possible to integrate the UAV Toolbox with [<u>MAVLink</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/uav/mavlink-support.html) and [<u>PX4® Autopilots</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/uav/basic-concepts-px4.html) for a variety of simulation, testing, and deployment applications. For more details, please explore the [<u>UAV Toolbox Documentation</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/uav/index.html).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BB2FE17A] ## Projects - MATLAB and Simulink Challenge Project Hub
%[text] This page contains a list of project ideas created by MathWorks experts that can be taken up to solve industry-relevant problems: 
%[text] [<u>**MATLAB and Simulink Challenge Project Hub**</u>](https://github.com/mathworks/MATLAB-Simulink-Challenge-Project-Hub)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DDC2A281] The following projects are designed especially for working with drones: [<u>**Project Ideas (Drones)**</u>](https://github.com/mathworks/MATLAB-Simulink-Challenge-Project-Hub/blob/main/megatrends/Drones.md)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CAB1B80D] We encourage students to take up final-year design projects to enhance their learning through hands-on practice.
%%
